+# Task: Windows `plan()` stays in the foreground; the w47 starter-test race (wave 50 c) Phase: followups Owner: builder w50c
+
+## Status
+
+Done (2026-10-23), not run on Windows (the OS family is patched to `win32` in the tests, as in
+waves 47 and 48). `plan()` refuses a trailing `&` and caps an over-limit timeout on a native-Windows
+host-local backend; its guidance texts there name WSL2 / Docker / SSH, not `background=true`. The
+w47 starter test waits for its two pid lines instead of sleeping 0.5 s. Changed outside the
+task's code and tests: `docs/user/getting-started/windows.md` (a "Foreground only" bullet; the
+`some-server &` example now reads as a script's own background program). Fail-first: the 13 new
+plan / tool tests fail on the base `terminal.py` (12 on behaviour, the sandbox/POSIX guard on the
+new `foreground_only` method); the base starter test fails 3 of 3 with the `IndexError` from wave 48
+when Git Bash's start is slowed by 1 s (`BASH_ENV` pointing at a `sleep 1` file). A first 20x run
+of the new test with the slowed start failed once (the 15 s wait timed out with nothing read): a
+second race, in the test's `GroupJobs.pipe_available` stand-in, which read "readable with 0 bytes
+counted" as end of file when the first bytes landed between its `FIONREAD` count and its `select`;
+the reader then quit early with no output (the same empty output the wave 48 failure showed). The
+stand-in now counts again when the pipe is readable (shown with a scratch script that writes into
+the pipe inside that window: the base stand-in answers None, the fixed one 5). After both fixes,
+20x each: the starter test 20/20; the whole `test_windows_rl26.py` 220/220 (every case uses the
+stand-in); the starter test with the 1 s slowed start 20/20, also under 12 CPU burners on 4 cores
+(and 80/80 more without them); `test_terminal_plan.py` 2000/2000.
+
+Read (reference @ `ab3fe9dffb0a89d534cb795e2a2940e5f6542056`): `tools/terminal_tool.py` (the
+foreground cap and its promotion to a tracked background job only, found by search; it has no
+native-Windows branch for either). Our own: `docs/notes/P25/windows-local.md` (P25-24 and the
+wave 47 / 48 sections, whose follow-up names this task), `docs/plan/phases/P25-hardening-parity-release.md` (P25-24
+row), `docs/plan/16-what-we-change.md` D-16.55, D-16.1043, D-16.1082. Evidence: none.
+
+What it does (own words): on POSIX, `plan()` quietly turns two foreground shapes into tracked
+background jobs: a command whose only backgrounding is one trailing `&`, and a foreground timeout
+above `terminal.max_foreground_timeout` (600 s). On native Windows with a host-local backend
+(`local`, which runs Git Bash there) background jobs cannot start (D-16.1043: `failed_start` with
+the WSL2 text) and the schema no longer offers them (D-16.1082, whose timeout text already says
+"maximum 600"). So both conversions there end in a refusal after approval, and the refusal texts
+from the foreground guidance tell the model to use `background=true`, which that host does not
+offer. On such a host `plan()` now:
+
+- a trailing `&`: refused before anything runs, with a foreground hint (run it without the `&`;
+  a server or watcher needs WSL2 or a Docker or SSH terminal backend);
+- a timeout above the cap: runs in the foreground with the timeout capped at the cap, and the
+  result says so (`timeout_capped`): what was asked, what was used, and that a longer job needs
+  WSL2 or a sandbox backend;
+- the other backgrounding shapes (inline `&`, nohup/disown/setsid, dev servers and watchers) are
+  still refused, with texts that name no `background=true`;
+- an explicit `background=true` keeps D-16.1043's refusal (D-16.1082 says so);
+- sandbox backends on Windows and every other OS are unchanged.
+
+Inputs → outputs: a `terminal` call + the scope's backend + the OS family → a `Plan` (foreground,
+capped) or a `Refusal`; the foreground result gains `timeout_capped` when the cap applied.
+
+Second part: `test_windows_starter_boots_and_stops_the_tree` (wave 47) slept 0.5 s and then read
+the first word of the output as the shell's pid; on a busy box the first `echo` had not arrived
+(failed once in 4 runs in wave 48). The test now waits until the reader thread has delivered the
+lines it needs (the shell's pid and its background child's pid), with a generous deadline, and
+also checks that the grandchild is gone, which is what the test name promises. The test's job
+stand-in (`GroupJobs.pipe_available`) had its own window (bytes arriving between its byte count and
+its `select` read as end of file); it now counts again before calling the pipe closed.
+
+Files: `src/indus/tools/builtin/terminal.py`, `tests/unit/tools/builtin/test_terminal_plan.py`,
+`tests/unit/backends/test_windows_rl26.py`, `docs/user/getting-started/windows.md`, this note.
+
+Edge cases / failure modes:
+- Help / version calls with a trailing `&` (`srv --help &`) are not a trailing-`&` job on POSIX
+  either; they fall to the guidance check exactly as on POSIX.
+- A timeout equal to the cap is not capped (no note).
+- A configured cap (`terminal.max_foreground_timeout`) is used, not the literal 600.
+- The backend is the scope's configured one, looked up in the call's environment registry (the
+  same rule as the schema override), so a Windows host whose profile uses Docker keeps POSIX
+  behaviour.
+- Test race: the reader thread appends chunks while the test polls; a chunk may hold a partial
+  line, so the wait counts complete lines.
+
+Our design differences / decisions:
+- `plan(call, scope, *, foreground_only=False)`; `foreground_guidance(command, *,
+  foreground_only=False)`; `TerminalTool.foreground_only(scope, environments)` shared with
+  `schema_overrides`. Our names.
+- Capping instead of refusing a long timeout: the offered schema already says "maximum 600" on
+  that host, and a command that finishes early returns at once, so running it is the useful
+  answer; the note tells the model a longer job was cut off (rc 124 at the cap).
+
+Tests we will write:
+- Plan: trailing `&` refused with a foreground hint naming WSL2 and no `background=true`; the
+  other backgrounding shapes refused without `background=true`; an over-cap timeout planned in
+  the foreground at the cap (configured cap honoured); an explicit `background=true` still plans
+  background; `foreground_only=False` unchanged.
+- Tool: on a patched `win32` host-local rig, `cmd &` is refused and nothing runs; an over-cap
+  call runs in the foreground with the timeout at the cap and the `timeout_capped` note; a
+  sandbox backend on `win32` still promotes.
+- Race: the starter test waits for its two pid lines; run 20 times in a row, also with Git
+  Bash's start slowed (`BASH_ENV`) and under CPU load.
+
+## Decisions for the lead
+- W50-c-1: on native Windows with a host-local terminal backend, `plan()` refuses a trailing `&`
+  (foreground hint, WSL2 / Docker / SSH for servers) instead of turning it into a background job.
+- W50-c-2: there, a foreground timeout above `terminal.max_foreground_timeout` is capped at it and
+  the result carries `timeout_capped`, instead of a promotion to a background job.
+- W50-c-3: there, the foreground guidance refusals (inline `&`, nohup/disown/setsid, servers and
+  watchers) name WSL2 or a Docker / SSH backend instead of `background=true`.
+- W50-c-4 (tests only): the wave 47 starter test waits for its two pid lines (a real signal,
+  15 s deadline) and checks the grandchild too; the `GroupJobs` stand-in counts the pipe again
+  before calling it closed.
+
+Compare checklist (filled after the rewrite; reopened the planning branch of `tools/terminal_tool.py`):
+- [x] Order kept: explicit background, then the trailing-`&` conversion, then the guidance
+  refusal, then the over-cap check; a trailing-`&` call is never also "promoted" (test:
+  `test_terminal_plan.py::test_trailing_amp_becomes_background`, `::test_overcap_foreground_is_promoted`,
+  both unchanged and passing)
+- [x] Over-cap promotion and the trailing-`&` conversion kept on POSIX and on Windows sandbox
+  backends (test: `::test_windows_sandbox_backend_and_posix_hosts_still_promote`)
+- [x] Windows host: trailing `&` refused before the backend is even opened (test:
+  `::test_windows_host_backend_refuses_trailing_amp_before_running`)
+- [x] Windows host: over-cap runs in the foreground at the cap with `timeout_capped` (tests:
+  `::test_foreground_only_caps_an_overcap_timeout_in_the_foreground`,
+  `::test_windows_host_backend_runs_an_overcap_call_in_the_foreground_at_the_cap`)
+- [x] Guidance texts on that host name no `background=true` (test:
+  `::test_foreground_only_guidance_names_no_background_option`)
+- [x] Help / version and ordinary `&&`, `2>&1` commands unchanged (test:
+  `::test_foreground_only_keeps_ordinary_and_explicit_background_plans`)
+- Deliberate differences: the reference has no native-Windows branch for either conversion; ours
+  follows D-16.1043 / D-16.1082 (no background jobs on a Windows host backend).
+- Not taken: a native port of background jobs (D-16.1043 stays); a refusal for an over-cap
+  timeout (capping is more useful, W50-c-2).
